WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
This antacid tablet is mostly calcium carbonate, a

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:04.200
base.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.880
So why not just titrate it with hydrochloric

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:07.360
acid?

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.920
Try it, and the tablet sits in the

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.600
flask, fizzing slowly, and the indicator changes colour

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.200
while solid is still left.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.840
A dark red wine vinegar has a different

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.280
problem: it reacts fast, but the red hides

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:22.400
the indicator's colour.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.680
So when a direct titration won't work, how

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.280
do you still find the amount?

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.860
Calcium carbonate barely dissolves in water, so the

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.140
acid can only attack the surface of the

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.100
solid, and that's slow.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.580
Add acid from a burette, and the solution

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.300
turns acidic around the solid before the rest

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:43.940
of it has reacted, so the indicator changes

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.660
too early, then drifts back as more solid

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:47.300
reacts.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.740
The carbon dioxide it makes also dissolves, as

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.260
carbonic acid, which blurs the endpoint even more.

00:00:55.467 --> 00:00:57.746
The fix is a back titration.

00:00:58.027 --> 00:01:01.306
Add a measured excess of standard hydrochloric acid,

00:01:01.387 --> 00:01:03.466
more than the tablet can use, and give

00:01:03.547 --> 00:01:05.786
it time to react completely.

00:01:05.867 --> 00:01:08.346
Then titrate the acid that's left over with

00:01:08.587 --> 00:01:10.586
standard sodium hydroxide.

00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:12.746
You can't easily measure the acid the tablet

00:01:12.827 --> 00:01:15.626
used, but you can measure what it left,

00:01:15.707 --> 00:01:17.946
and the difference is the acid it used.

00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:21.966
One step comes before that second titration.

00:01:22.527 --> 00:01:25.246
Some of the carbon dioxide stays dissolved, as

00:01:25.327 --> 00:01:26.286
carbonic acid.

00:01:26.927 --> 00:01:29.806
Sodium hydroxide would react with it too, so

00:01:29.847 --> 00:01:31.806
the leftover acid would look bigger than it

00:01:31.887 --> 00:01:34.286
is, and the calcium carbonate would come out

00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:35.166
too small.

00:01:35.807 --> 00:01:38.286
So the flask is boiled gently first, to

00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:41.486
drive the carbon dioxide off, then cooled.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.620
Here's one tablet, worked through.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:48.380
Step one, the acid added: n equals c

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:51.260
V, zero point zero one two five zero

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:52.380
moles.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:55.180
Step two, the acid left over.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:58.300
Sodium hydroxide reacts with hydrochloric acid one to

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.260
one, so it's the moles of sodium hydroxide

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:04.220
in the titre: zero point zero zero four

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:05.900
six zero moles.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:09.100
Notice you subtract moles, not volumes, because the

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.660
two solutions have different concentrations.

00:02:12.867 --> 00:02:13.586
Step three:

00:02:14.147 --> 00:02:17.266
the tablet used the difference, zero point zero

00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:20.226
zero seven nine zero moles of acid.

00:02:20.307 --> 00:02:23.826
Step four: the equation says each calcium carbonate

00:02:23.907 --> 00:02:27.586
uses two hydrochloric acid, so halve it, zero

00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:30.866
point zero zero three nine five moles.

00:02:31.027 --> 00:02:33.266
Times the molar mass gives zero point three

00:02:33.387 --> 00:02:35.986
nine five grams, which is sixty three point

00:02:36.027 --> 00:02:37.666
eight per cent of the tablet.

00:02:37.707 --> 00:02:40.146
The rest is other ingredients, like the binder.

00:02:41.367 --> 00:02:42.966
Now the red wine vinegar.

00:02:43.207 --> 00:02:46.646
Its ethanoic acid dissolves and reacts quickly, so

00:02:46.687 --> 00:02:49.366
a direct titration would work, if you could

00:02:49.447 --> 00:02:50.806
see the endpoint.

00:02:50.967 --> 00:02:53.526
So instead of an indicator, use a conductivity

00:02:53.607 --> 00:02:57.126
meter, which doesn't care about colour, or cloudiness.

00:02:57.167 --> 00:02:58.966
In the last video, you saw why the

00:02:59.047 --> 00:03:01.046
readings fall to a V for a strong

00:03:01.207 --> 00:03:03.606
acid, and bend for a weak acid like

00:03:03.687 --> 00:03:04.166
this one.

00:03:05.367 --> 00:03:08.566
Add the sodium hydroxide in equal portions, read

00:03:08.647 --> 00:03:11.286
the meter after each, and plot the points.

00:03:11.607 --> 00:03:15.206
Before equivalence, each portion makes the same change,

00:03:15.287 --> 00:03:18.406
turning some ethanoic acid into sodium and ethanoate

00:03:18.487 --> 00:03:20.966
ions, so the points climb in a nearly

00:03:21.047 --> 00:03:22.406
straight line.

00:03:22.567 --> 00:03:25.526
After equivalence, each portion adds the same extra

00:03:25.607 --> 00:03:28.486
hydroxide: a steeper straight line.

00:03:28.567 --> 00:03:30.966
The first points curve a little, and equivalence

00:03:31.047 --> 00:03:34.086
usually falls between two readings, so don't hunt

00:03:34.167 --> 00:03:35.606
for one point.

00:03:35.727 --> 00:03:38.086
Draw the two best-fit lines, and read where

00:03:38.167 --> 00:03:38.886
they cross.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:41.460
There's one catch.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:45.220
Each portion also adds volume, which dilutes every

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:47.060
ion and bends the lines.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:49.540
So the vinegar is first topped up with

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:50.660
plenty of water.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:53.460
That doesn't change the moles of acid, but

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:56.500
now each portion changes the total volume by

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:57.940
only a small fraction.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:01.460
And the sodium hydroxide is concentrated, so only

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:03.060
a small volume goes in.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:05.860
Or each reading can be corrected for the

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:06.900
volume added.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:11.100
Here, ten point zero zero millilitres of the

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:14.020
vinegar was topped up with water and titrated

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.580
with one point zero zero moles per litre

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:17.940
sodium hydroxide.

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:20.740
The lines cross at eight point two zero

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:22.980
millilitres, the equivalence volume.

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:26.180
So the acid is zero point zero zero

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:28.020
eight two zero moles.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:31.060
Divide by the ten millilitres of vinegar, not

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:33.460
the diluted volume, because the water added no

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:36.660
acid: zero point eight two zero moles per

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:39.060
litre, with no indicator at all.

00:04:40.267 --> 00:04:41.706
So here's what to remember.

00:04:42.267 --> 00:04:45.466
Back titration: total minus leftover.

00:04:45.947 --> 00:04:48.266
It works because the only acid the tablet

00:04:48.347 --> 00:04:50.106
can have used is the acid that's no

00:04:50.187 --> 00:04:51.066
longer there.

00:04:51.307 --> 00:04:54.186
And for a conductivity titration, read where the

00:04:54.347 --> 00:04:55.306
lines cross.

00:04:56.534 --> 00:04:57.453
Your turn.

00:04:57.734 --> 00:05:00.773
A zero point five zero zero gram antacid

00:05:00.854 --> 00:05:04.693
tablet containing aluminium hydroxide is added to forty

00:05:04.774 --> 00:05:08.293
point zero zero millilitres of zero point four

00:05:08.494 --> 00:05:11.973
zero zero zero moles per litre hydrochloric acid,

00:05:12.054 --> 00:05:13.053
an excess.

00:05:13.414 --> 00:05:16.133
The leftover acid needs twenty one point six

00:05:16.254 --> 00:05:19.573
zero millilitres of zero point two zero zero

00:05:19.694 --> 00:05:22.213
zero moles per litre sodium hydroxide.

00:05:22.934 --> 00:05:25.733
Calculate the percentage of aluminium hydroxide in the

00:05:25.814 --> 00:05:27.493
tablet, by mass.

00:05:27.814 --> 00:05:29.093
Pause here to answer.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:41.980
The tablet used the acid added minus the

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:45.340
acid left: zero point zero one one six

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:46.340
eight moles.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:50.620
Each aluminium hydroxide uses three hydrochloric acid, so

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:53.660
divide by three, then multiply by seventy eight

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:56.940
point zero zero: zero point three zero four

00:05:57.100 --> 00:06:00.140
grams, or sixty point seven per cent.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.060
Forget the three, and you'd get a hundred

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:04.460
and eighty two per cent, which is impossible

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:05.660
for part of a tablet.

00:06:06.867 --> 00:06:07.506
Last one.

00:06:08.147 --> 00:06:11.026
Zero point one zero zero moles per litre

00:06:11.107 --> 00:06:14.146
sulfuric acid is added to fifty point zero

00:06:14.307 --> 00:06:17.866
zero millilitres of barium hydroxide solution, and the

00:06:17.907 --> 00:06:19.906
conductivity is measured.

00:06:20.027 --> 00:06:23.586
Explain why the conductivity falls almost to zero,

00:06:23.827 --> 00:06:26.386
then use the graph to calculate the concentration

00:06:26.467 --> 00:06:28.306
of the barium hydroxide.

00:06:28.467 --> 00:06:29.746
Pause here to answer.

00:06:40.167 --> 00:06:43.206
Hydroxide is removed as water, and barium is

00:06:43.287 --> 00:06:47.046
removed as solid barium sulfate, so at equivalence

00:06:47.247 --> 00:06:49.206
almost no ions are left to carry a

00:06:49.407 --> 00:06:50.166
current.

00:06:50.327 --> 00:06:53.686
After that, excess acid adds ions again.

00:06:53.767 --> 00:06:56.566
The lines cross at twelve point five millilitres,

00:06:56.607 --> 00:06:58.726
and the ratio is one to one, so

00:06:58.807 --> 00:07:01.686
the concentration is zero point zero two five

00:07:01.807 --> 00:07:03.766
zero moles per litre.

00:07:03.927 --> 00:07:06.566
Reading the lowest point, twelve point zero, is

00:07:06.607 --> 00:07:07.286
the slip.

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:11.700
So when a direct titration won't work: if

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:14.820
the sample is an insoluble or slow solid,

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:15.900
back titrate.

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:18.900
Add a measured excess, let the sample react,

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:20.540
and titrate what is left.

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:23.460
If the reaction makes carbon dioxide, drive it

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:25.700
off and cool the flask before the back

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:26.380
titre.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:29.340
Subtract moles and use the reaction ratio.

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:32.580
If colour or cloudiness hides the endpoint, use

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:35.060
a conductivity meter, and read where the lines

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:35.620
cross.

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:40.420
Next: practice with curves, indicators and back titrations.

00:07:41.634 --> 00:07:43.073
Here are the key notes.

00:07:43.314 --> 00:07:45.233
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:45.314 --> 00:07:46.113
to copy them.
